<!-- livebook:{"break_markdown":true} -->

You never call `Phoenix.PubSub.broadcast` after a write. You declare on the resource which actions publish, on which topic, and Ash publishes after the transaction commits, for every caller. This is the same mechanism `ash_a2ui` (lesson 23) uses to refresh surfaces, and the one `ash_strangler` (lesson 24) bridges legacy writes into.

```elixir
Phoenix.PubSub.Supervisor.start_link(name: Tutorial.PubSub)

defmodule Tutorial.Endpoint do
  # Ash.Notifier.PubSub needs a module with broadcast/3; an endpoint has one.
  def broadcast(topic, event, payload),
    do: Phoenix.PubSub.broadcast(Tutorial.PubSub, topic, %{event: event, payload: payload})
end
```

Here is the lesson 11 domain again, self-contained (its `string_length` validation included), with two additions: `notifiers: [Ash.Notifier.PubSub]` on the resource, and a `pub_sub` block that says *which* actions publish on *which* topics. Both are declarations — no broadcast call anywhere:

```elixir
defmodule Tutorial.Support.Ticket do
  use Ash.Resource,
    domain: Tutorial.Support,
    data_layer: Ash.DataLayer.Ets,
    authorizers: [Ash.Policy.Authorizer],
    notifiers: [Ash.Notifier.PubSub]

  attributes do
    uuid_primary_key :id
    attribute :subject, :string, allow_nil?: false, public?: true
    attribute :status, :atom, constraints: [one_of: [:open, :closed]], default: :open, public?: true
    attribute :owner_id, :uuid, public?: true
    create_timestamp :inserted_at, public?: true
  end

  actions do
    defaults [:read]

    create :open do
      accept [:subject, :owner_id]
      validate string_length(:subject, min: 5)
    end

    update :close, change: set_attribute(:status, :closed)

    read :open_tickets do
      filter expr(status == :open)
      pagination keyset?: true, default_limit: 20, required?: false
    end
  end

  policies do
    policy action_type(:read) do
      authorize_if expr(owner_id == ^actor(:id))
    end

    policy action_type([:create, :update]) do
      authorize_if actor_present()
    end
  end

  pub_sub do
    module Tutorial.Endpoint
    prefix "tickets"
    publish_all :create, ["created"]
    publish_all :update, ["updated"]
  end
end

defmodule Tutorial.Support do
  use Ash.Domain

  resources do
    resource Tutorial.Support.Ticket do
      define :open_ticket, action: :open, args: [:subject]
      define :list_open, action: :open_tickets
      define :close_ticket, action: :close
    end
  end
end
```

(If you already edited lesson 11's cell to add these, that cell also works — but re-run *that* cell; defining the module twice is a compile error, see lesson 10.)

## Subscribe in a test process first

Subscribe the notebook to `"tickets:created"`, open a ticket, and `receive` the notification.

**Enter your solution**

```elixir

```

<details class="rounded-lg my-4" style="background-color: #96ef86; color: #040604;">
  <summary class="cursor-pointer font-bold p-4">Show Solution</summary>
  <div class="p-4">

  ```elixir
  Phoenix.PubSub.subscribe(Tutorial.PubSub, "tickets:created")
  actor = %{id: Ash.UUID.generate()}
  Tutorial.Support.open_ticket!("Live!", %{owner_id: actor.id}, actor: actor)

  receive do
    %{event: "open", payload: %Ash.Notifier.Notification{data: t}} -> t.subject
  after
    1000 -> :nothing
  end
  ```

  The notification's `event` is the action's **name** (`:open`), not its type (`create`) — `publish_all :create, [...]` selects actions *by type*, but the notification names the action that ran. Match `"open"`, or bind the variable and inspect it.

  </div>
</details>

## In a LiveView: `connected?/1` first

Subscribe only when connected; otherwise the static render subscribes a process that is about to die. Re-read through the action when a notification arrives, rather than trusting the payload, so policies apply to the update too.

```elixir
alice = %{id: Ash.UUID.generate()}

defmodule LiveTicketsLive do
  use Phoenix.LiveView

  # Baked in at compile time, in this process. The notebook's process
  # dictionary is invisible to the LiveView process: mount/3 runs elsewhere,
  # so Process.get(:demo_actor) there is always nil (and the read policy then
  # 403s). A real app carries the actor in the session instead.
  @demo_actor alice

  def mount(_params, _session, socket) do
    if connected?(socket), do: Phoenix.PubSub.subscribe(Tutorial.PubSub, "tickets:created")
    {:ok, assign(socket, current_user: @demo_actor)}
  end

  def handle_params(_params, _uri, socket) do
    tickets = Tutorial.Support.list_open!(actor: socket.assigns.current_user)
    {:noreply, stream(socket, :tickets, tickets, reset: true)}
  end

  def handle_info(%{payload: %Ash.Notifier.Notification{data: %{id: id}}}, socket) do
    case Ash.get(Tutorial.Support.Ticket, id, actor: socket.assigns.current_user) do
      {:ok, t} -> {:noreply, stream_insert(socket, :tickets, t, at: 0)}
      _not_visible -> {:noreply, socket}
    end
  end

  def render(assigns) do
    ~H"""
    <ul id="tickets" phx-update="stream">
      <li :for={{id, t} <- @streams.tickets} id={id}>{t.subject}</li>
    </ul>
    """
  end
end

PhoenixPlayground.start(live: LiveTicketsLive, port: 4012, open_browser: false)
```

Open http://localhost:4012, then create tickets for Alice and for someone else from the notebook. Only Alice's appear.

## Exercise

Make closed tickets disappear live. Publish `:close` on `"tickets:closed"` and `stream_delete` on receipt.

**Enter your solution**

```elixir

```

<details class="rounded-lg my-4" style="background-color: #96ef86; color: #040604;">
  <summary class="cursor-pointer font-bold p-4">Show Solution</summary>
  <div class="p-4">

  Resource: `publish :close, ["closed"]`. LiveView: subscribe to `"tickets:closed"` too and

  ```elixir
  def handle_info(%{event: "close", payload: %{data: t}}, socket),
    do: {:noreply, stream_delete(socket, :tickets, t)}
  ```

  (Put this clause above the general one.) Deleting needs no re-read: removing a row reveals nothing.

  </div>
</details>

